  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it in a browser or build it with `vite build`, so the GLSL and the rendered look are untested. A shader compile error would log `CUSTOM_SHADER_COMPILE_FAILED` and skip that draw.\n\n- **Water:** a `material.custom` shader on a 240×240 grid, denser near the camera.\n  - Four directional sine waves displace the vertices in the vertex shader, and the normal is computed analytically.\n  - The fragment shader adds noise ripples from two scrolling `a3dFbm` layers, faded with distance.\n  - Schlick fresnel with F0 0.02 mixes the water body colour with the reflected sky, so grazing angles reflect more sky.\n  - The sun glint is a sharp lobe plus a broad one.\n  - The water fades into the horizon colour at distance.\n- **Sky:** a sphere around the camera, drawn from the inside with `side: \"double\"`, `depthWrite: false` and `fog: false`. It has a horizon-to-zenith gradient, sun glow and disc, and drifting fbm clouds. The water shader uses the same sky function for its reflection, so the two match.\n- **Time:** a `waveClock` uniform is set on both nodes every frame in `app.onFrame`.\n- **Colour:** colours are authored in sRGB and decoded with `a3dSrgbToLinear`. The app uses `output: { toneMapping: \"none\" }`, as the custom-shader docs prescribe, to avoid washed-out or dark output.\n- **Camera and contract:** the camera sits at y=3.2 and looks toward the horizon. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves.\n\nI used `.then` instead of top-level `await`, because Vite's default build target may not accept it.\n\n",
